In [[music theory]], an '''interval''' is a difference in [[pitch (music)|pitch]] between two sounds.<ref>{{Cite book| last = Prout | first = Ebenezer|author-link=Ebenezer Prout| title=Harmony, Its Theory and Practice|url=https://archive.org/details/harmonyitstheory0000prou/page/n3/mode/2up|via=[[Internet Archive]]|location=London; Boston|publisher=[[Augener & Co.]], The Boston Music Co.| edition = 31st | year = 1903 | chapter = I. Introduction | page =[https://archive.org/details/harmonyitstheory0000prou/page/1/mode/1up 1]}}</ref> An interval may be described as '''horizontal''', '''linear''', or '''melodic''' if it refers to successively sounding tones, such as two adjacent pitches in a melody, and '''vertical''' or '''harmonic''' if it pertains to simultaneously sounding tones, such as in a [[Chord (music)|chord]].<ref name="LindleyMarkCampbell">{{Cite Grove |last1=Lindley |first1=Mark |last2=Campbell |first2=Murray |last3=Greated |first3=Clive |title=Interval}}</ref><ref>{{Citation|last1 = Aldwell | first1 = E.| last2 = Schachter | first2 = C. | last3 = Cadwallader | first3 = A. |title = Harmony and Voice Leading | edition = 4th | chapter = Part 1: The Primary Materials and Procedures, Unit 1 | date = 11 March 2010 | page = 8 | publisher = Schirmer | isbn = 978-0495189756}}</ref>

In [[Western culture#Music|Western]] music, intervals are most commonly differencing between [[musical note|notes]] of a [[diatonic scale]]. Intervals between successive notes of a scale are also known as scale steps. The smallest of these intervals is a [[semitone]]. Intervals smaller than a semitone are called [[microtone]]s. They can be formed using the notes of various kinds of non-diatonic scales. Some of the very smallest ones are called [[Comma (music)|commas]], and describe small discrepancies, observed in some [[tuning system]]s, between [[enharmonic|enharmonically equivalent]] notes such as C{{music|#}} and D{{music|flat}}. Intervals can be arbitrarily small, and even imperceptible to the human ear.

In physical terms, an interval is the [[Interval ratio|ratio]] between two sonic frequencies. For example, any two notes an octave apart have a frequency ratio of 2:1. This means that successive increments of pitch by the same interval result in an exponential increase of frequency, even though the human ear perceives this as a linear increase in pitch. For this reason, intervals are often measured in [[Cent (music)|cents]], a unit derived from the [[logarithm]] of the frequency ratio.

In Western music theory, the most common naming scheme for intervals describes two properties of the interval: the [[#Quality|quality]] (perfect, major, minor, augmented, diminished) and [[#Number|number]] (unison, second, third, etc.). Examples include the [[minor third]] or [[perfect fifth]]. These names identify not only the difference in semitones between the upper and lower notes but also how the interval is [[enharmonic spelling|spelled]]. The importance of spelling stems from the historical practice of differentiating the frequency ratios of enharmonic intervals such as G–G{{music|#}} and G–A{{music|b}}.<ref>{{Citation | last = Duffin | first = Ross W. | title=How Equal Temperament Ruined Harmony (and Why You Should Care) | publisher = W. W. Norton | edition = 1st | year = 2007 | chapter = 3. Non-keyboard tuning | isbn = 978-0-393-33420-3}}</ref>

